  "articleSummary": "The article is a practical tutorial for self-hosters, arguing that Docker security is the operator’s responsibility whether services run on a home network or public cloud. It covers deliberate image updates and version pinning, secure secret storage, canary tokens, read-only volumes, firewall rules, network segmentation, access controls, authenticated Nginx proxies, and monitoring or verification. The tone is approachable and mildly playful, using framing such as the “`:latest` Dance,” “For the brave,” and keeping out “riff-raff,” while offering techniques users can choose according to their risk tolerance and setup.",
